TOP DEVSECOPS Interview Questions 

1) Difference between terraform import and terraform taint
terraform import: Brings existing infrastructure (e.g., an already created AWS EC2) into Terraform state so Terraform can manage it.
terraform taint: Marks a resource for destruction and recreation during the next apply.
👉 Example:
Import: terraform import aws_instance.myec2
Taint: terraform taint aws_instance.myec2

2) Managing Secrets in Terraform (without hardcoding)
Use Terraform variables with sensitive = true.
Store secrets in AWS Secrets Manager, SSM Parameter Store.
Access them via data sources or the external-secrets provider.
Never commit secrets in .tf files or state—use remote backends with encryption.

3) Difference between count and for_each
count: Creates multiple resources using index numbers (count.index). Best when you want identical resources.
for_each: Creates resources mapped to keys in a map/set. Best when each resource is unique.
👉 Real-world:
count: Create 3 EC2 instances with same config.
for_each: Create EC2 instances with different tags or sizes.

4) Drift Detection in Terraform
Run terraform plan regularly. It shows if real infra has drifted from state.
Use CI/CD pipelines to run plans automatically.
Some backends (Terraform Cloud, Atlantis) also provide drift detection alerts.

5) Terraform Remote Backend
A remote backend stores state file in a central, shared location (e.g., S3 + DynamoDB, Terraform Cloud, Azure Storage).
Why important?
Enables collaboration.
Avoids local state conflicts.
Provides locking (prevents two users from applying at same time).

6) Managing Multiple Environments
Use workspaces (terraform workspace new dev).
Or separate state files and directories (dev/, staging/, prod/).
Or use Terragrunt for managing environments with DRY (don’t repeat yourself).

7) Difference between local-exec and remote-exec provisioners
local-exec: Runs a command on the machine where Terraform is executed.
remote-exec: Runs a command on the created resource (via SSH/WinRM).
👉 Example:
local-exec: echo "Instance created" > log.txt
remote-exec: sudo apt update on the new EC2.

8) Safe Rollback After Failed Deployment
Use Terraform state to revert changes.
If deployment fails:
Run terraform apply again (idempotent).
Use version-controlled modules (roll back Git version).
Restore from remote state backup if state is corrupted.

9) terraform refresh vs terraform plan
refresh: Updates Terraform state with real-world infra, but doesn’t show changes to be applied.
plan: Shows the difference between current state vs desired configuration.
👉 In latest versions (>= 0.15), terraform refresh is deprecated and integrated with plan and apply.

10) Writing Reusable Terraform Modules
Create a module directory with main.tf, variables.tf, outputs.tf.
Use input variables for flexibility and outputs for sharing info.
Example: A VPC module reused across dev, staging, prod.
Keep modules in a separate repo or Terraform Registry for sharing.
